# Upload filter for `railway up`, which runs from the REPO ROOT.
#
# Why this file exists at the root
# --------------------------------
# The Railway service applies its own Root Directory (`backend`) to whatever
# tree is uploaded, so `scripts/ops/deploy_backend.sh` uploads from here rather
# than from `backend/` (uploading from inside `backend/` makes the builder look
# for `backend/backend/` and the build fails outright).
#
# That means `backend/.railwayignore` is no longer the file governing the
# upload -- the CLI reads the ignore file at the directory it is uploading
# from. Without this file a deploy from a working checkout would try to tar the
# whole repo: ~858M of `frontend/node_modules` plus ~489M of `backend/.venv`,
# around 5G in total, which is slow at best and fails mid-stream at worst. A
# fresh clone has neither, so this only bites on a real developer machine --
# exactly where deploys are run from.
#
# The builder only ever reads `backend/`, so everything below is either
# reproducible from a lockfile or irrelevant to the backend image.

# Frontend: deployed separately on Vercel, never part of the backend image.
frontend/

# Python environments and caches -- recreated by `uv sync` in the builder.
.venv/
venv/
__pycache__/
*.pyc
*.pyo
.pytest_cache/
.mypy_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.coverage
htmlcov/

# Node tooling that can appear at the root.
node_modules/

# Database dumps: encrypted customer data, and never needed to build.
backend/backups/
*.dump
*.dump.enc

# Git metadata. Excluded deliberately -- see the build-stamp note in
# scripts/ops/deploy_backend.sh: no git data reaches the builder, which is why
# the deployed SHA ships as backend/app/build_info.json instead.
.git/
.github/

# Local agent/editor state.
.ezcoder/
.vscode/
.idea/
.DS_Store

# Local env files. Railway injects real config as service variables; shipping a
# local .env into the image would both leak secrets and override them.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
